<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Int J Emerg Med</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>International Journal of Emergency Medicine</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">1865-1372</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1865-1380</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Springer-Verlag</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">20157457</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2700231</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">95</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/s12245-009-0095-x</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Clinical Images</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Listening to syncope</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sogunuru</surname><given-names>Guruprasad</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Vuddanda</surname><given-names>Kishan</given-names></name><address><email>Kishanvuddanda@gmail.com</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff2">2</xref></contrib><aff id="Aff1"><label>1</label>Kishanvuddanda, S/o V. Dhananjaya, 13-07-939 G/A, Korlagunta, Tirupathi, Chittoor district, Andhra Pradesh India </aff><aff id="Aff2"><label>2</label>Kishanvuddanda, C/O DBR hospitals, Mosque road, Tirupathi, Chittoor district, Andhra Pradesh India </aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>17</day><month>3</month><year>2009</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>17</day><month>3</month><year>2009</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>6</month><year>2009</year></pub-date><volume>2</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>125</fpage><lpage>126</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>21</day><month>12</month><year>2008</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>3</day><month>2</month><year>2009</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; Springer-Verlag London Ltd 2009</copyright-statement></permissions><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>issue-copyright-statement</meta-name><meta-value>&#x000a9; Springer-Verlag London Ltd 2009</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><p>A 35-year-old man presented with progressive exertional dyspnea (3&#x000a0;months) and syncopal attacks (1&#x000a0;week). He had tachypnea, tachycardia and hypotension. Examination revealed a mid-diastolic plop at the apex. Clinical suspicion was a left atrial mass with a ball valve-like mechanism (LA thrombus or LA tumors). Echo and cardiac MRIs with gadolinium enhancement (to differentiate between a tumor and thrombus) were considered as necessary investigations. An emergency echo revealed a large mass (8.0&#x02009;&#x000d7;&#x02009;3.5&#x000a0;cm) in the left atrium arising from the roof. It was prolapsing through the mitral valve, compromising LV inflow. There were no embolic manifestations or features of NAME (nevi, atrial myxoma, myxoid neurofibromas, ephelides) or LAMB (lentigenes, atrial myxoma, blue nevi) syndromes or Carney&#x02019;s complex. The patient underwent surgery. The excised mass was conical, and histopathology revealed myxomatous areas with vascular channels and cellular areas. This was diagnosed as a cellular variant of angiomyxoma (a rare variant). The patient recovered with no complications and was discharged after 1&#x000a0;week of observation. Atrial myxoma is the most common benign primary tumor of the heart. The majority of them are solitary and occur in the left atrium in the zone of the fossa ovalis. Common symptoms are dyspnea and syncope. Other presentations include PUO, weight loss, embolic manifestations and arrhythmias. Definitive treatment is always surgery. The present case emphasizes the clinical significance of cardiac auscultation in a patient who presents with syncope.<fig position="anchor" id="Fig1"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;1</label><caption><p>Echo (apical 4 chamber view) showing a large left atrial mass prolapsing through the mitral orifice</p></caption><graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="12245_2009_95_Figa_HTML" id="MO1"/></fig><fig position="anchor" id="Fig2"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;2</label><caption><p>Cut section of resected LA mass showing the glistening central myxomatous areas</p></caption><graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="12245_2009_95_Figb_HTML" id="MO2"/></fig><fig position="anchor" id="Fig3"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;3</label><caption><p>LA mass histopathology (H &#x00026; E) showing vascular channels in the centre along with cellular areas consistent with cellular variant of angiomyxoma</p></caption><graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="12245_2009_95_Figc_HTML" id="MO3"/></fig></p></body></article>